    {
      "url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/v3.8.1/guides/dtypes",
      "title": "Transformers.js 3.8.1 quantization dtypes",
      "excerpt": "The list of available quantizations depends on the model, but some common ones are: full-precision ( \"fp32\" ), half-precision ( \"fp16\" ), 8-bit ( \"q8\" , \"int8\" , \"uint8\" ), and 4-bit ( \"q4\" , \"bnb4\" , \"q4f16\" ). (e.g., mixedbread-ai/mxbai-embed-xsmall-v1) Basic usage Example: Run Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct in 4-bit quantization ( demo ) Copied import { pipeline } from \"@huggingface/transformers\" ; // Create a text generation pipeline const generator = await pipeline ( \"text-generation\" , \"onnx-community/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\" , { dtype : \"q4\" , device : \"webgpu\" }, ); // Define the list of messages",
      "excerptHash": "d6b691d4fbab4fe50ea024070a4274664af0aaaa60f0ec5f765a7dd2fb1f765d",
      "kind": "official_document_excerpt",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17T08:26:15.236437+00:00",
      "source": "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers.js/v3.8.1/guides/dtypes",
      "sourceHash": "38394c0db18d12c2400bc1361d338d4a2241b790faaaa4e3970506b8e30a1bd9"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17338",
      "title": "When Should LLM Agents Forget? Memory Filtering Under Query-Critical Resolver Errors",
      "excerpt": "arXiv:2611.17338v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: LLM memory systems often delete facts classified as stale. We show that this hard-filtering step can reduce downstream accuracy when resolver errors delete query-critical facts. Across three models, a controlled sensitivity intervention moves hard-drop from gains of +0.186 to +0.230 at perfect resolution to losses of -0.176 to -0.181 at the lowest tested reliability. Paired targeted interventions show that false-drop causes significantly greater degradation than false-keep, while a non-deleting baseline can still fail when incorrect labels steer ",
      "excerptHash": "2ed25ee0c694c42f88ee4a4ca2957cee599e4ee958a9ef038c6a53ca39e5eff6",
      "kind": "abstract_excerpt",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17T08:26:15.308793+00:00",
      "source": "https://rss.arxiv.org/rss/cs.AI",
      "sourceHash": "957d42abad424e475a5d61967825fcac340915ebe4003b9616aa21f2369d9be7"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17339",
      "title": "Does the Ranking Hold? A Practitioner-Facing Audit of Hallucination Detectors on Small, Quantized, On-Device LLMs",
      "excerpt": "arXiv:2611.17339v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Hallucination detection for large language models is dominated by methods evaluated on 7B-70B research models with abundant sampling budgets and exact-match or ROUGE correctness labels. The small models practitioners actually ship on-device, Phi-3-mini, Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct, and Gemma-2-2B-Instruct, typically at 4-bit precision, sit outside this evidence base. We present a reproducible one-GPU audit of four detectors spanning three paradigms: length-normalized token-margin calibration, a lexical n-gram baseline, and two samp",
      "excerptHash": "77ad7c4af3198f0231b8b048e32cb2b2bff3a2d18da5f750a37670e98ad8e8a4",
      "kind": "abstract_excerpt",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17T08:26:15.309230+00:00",
      "source": "https://rss.arxiv.org/rss/cs.AI",
      "sourceHash": "957d42abad424e475a5d61967825fcac340915ebe4003b9616aa21f2369d9be7"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17340",
      "title": "LoRAWeaver: Directional Adapter Routing in LoRA Representation Space",
      "excerpt": "arXiv:2611.17340v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Large language models are expensive to update through full retraining. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning reduces the cost of adaptation, but repeated adaptation creates a growing pool of task-specific LoRA adapters. This pool complicates adapter selection during inference. Existing routing and encoder-based methods often require retraining when new adapters are added, which limits their use in dynamic settings. This paper treats adapter routing as a representation alignment problem in the adapter-output space. The proposed training-free method sele",
      "excerptHash": "42ed1483d81d8e552f252a18eb8445736f130cdfadcb6d153c2332481c3294cf",
      "kind": "abstract_excerpt",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17T08:26:15.309647+00:00",
      "source": "https://rss.arxiv.org/rss/cs.AI",
      "sourceHash": "957d42abad424e475a5d61967825fcac340915ebe4003b9616aa21f2369d9be7"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17341",
      "title": "Tabular Population Priors as Structured Retrieval for EHR Medication Set Prediction",
      "excerpt": "arXiv:2611.17341v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Medication prediction from electronic health records is a fixed vocabulary structured prediction problem, yet recent clinical RAG systems often treat it like open ended case based reasoning: retrieve similar patient admissions and ask an LLM to infer a medication set. This retrieval object is indirect the model receives retrieved patient records while the target is a discrete set of medication class labels. We propose a more task aligned retrieval object: a tabular population prior ranked medication class candidates from a sparse condition medica",
      "excerptHash": "c59449f0f34b2061dd6fed2f895ee6d48ceaf4db476d2e2a52236d0b57548ecd",
      "kind": "abstract_excerpt",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17T08:26:15.310082+00:00",
      "source": "https://rss.arxiv.org/rss/cs.AI",
      "sourceHash": "957d42abad424e475a5d61967825fcac340915ebe4003b9616aa21f2369d9be7"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17495",
      "title": "An Operationalization of Opaque Serial Depth",
      "excerpt": "arXiv:2611.17495v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Chain-of-thought (CoT) is currently a valuable tool for overseeing AI models, but some architectural shifts could significantly reduce CoT monitorability. We operationalize a measure that serves as a proxy for the amount of unverbalized serial cognition a model can perform, as a specific instantiation of the notion of opaque serial depth: roughly, the length of the longest path through a model's computation that does not pass through an interpretable bottleneck. Our main contribution is a particular standard for what counts as such a bottleneck. ",
      "excerptHash": "f248246883fe874aadc6ac0b5176189e27fb0c0800c7911954828d01fe195a94",
      "kind": "abstract_excerpt",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17T08:26:15.310513+00:00",
      "source": "https://rss.arxiv.org/rss/cs.AI",
      "sourceHash": "957d42abad424e475a5d61967825fcac340915ebe4003b9616aa21f2369d9be7"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17501",
      "title": "Advancing Quantization for R&R Models with Microscaling Formats",
      "excerpt": "arXiv:2611.17501v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: Recommendation and Ranking (R&R) models, particularly in digital advertising, require massive inference throughput but exhibit extreme sensitivity to precision loss. Unlike Large Language Models (LLMs) that frequently tolerate quantization-induced accuracy drops of 1% or more, production-grade R&R models enforce a stringent launch bar: model quality must remain within a 0.01% Normalized Entropy (NE) margin relative to the baseline, which commonly uses FP16 or FP32. This tight constraint has historically hindered the adoption of aggressive quantiz",
      "excerptHash": "0e15b70a25b82aaa81fcffd00a0617393bbc3de86d4ba70343807854cc5d488e",
      "kind": "abstract_excerpt",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17T08:26:15.310965+00:00",
      "source": "https://rss.arxiv.org/rss/cs.AI",
      "sourceHash": "957d42abad424e475a5d61967825fcac340915ebe4003b9616aa21f2369d9be7"
    }
